WEBVTT

00:00.560 --> 00:03.445
We are asked to
graph the function y

00:03.445 --> 00:07.940
is equal to negative
2.5 cosine of 1/3

00:07.940 --> 00:12.150
x on the interval, 0 to 6
pi, including the endpoints.

00:12.150 --> 00:15.610
So let me do my best
attempt at graphing that.

00:15.610 --> 00:18.890
And to start off, I'm going
to graph with the simplest

00:18.890 --> 00:21.390
function, or the
simplest version of this,

00:21.390 --> 00:24.690
or the root of this,
which is just cosine of x.

00:24.690 --> 00:27.290
So let me just graph,
and eventually you

00:27.290 --> 00:31.760
can kind of-- let me
just graph cosine of x.

00:31.760 --> 00:33.880
So that's my y-axis.

00:33.880 --> 00:36.130
And I want to have some space
here so I can eventually

00:36.130 --> 00:39.380
graph this entire thing.

00:39.380 --> 00:43.710
So let's say that this is
negative 1, this is negative 2.

00:43.710 --> 00:47.970
This is positive 1,
this is positive 2.

00:47.970 --> 00:54.740
And let's say that this
right over here is 2 pi.

00:54.740 --> 00:57.587
And then of course that
could be pi right over there.

00:57.587 --> 00:59.170
Now, the first thing
I'm going to do--

00:59.170 --> 01:02.340
let me copy this because
I could use it later

01:02.340 --> 01:03.720
to graph the whole thing.

01:06.510 --> 01:07.920
So let's start off.

01:07.920 --> 01:12.400
So I'm just going to graph
y is equal to cosine of x.

01:12.400 --> 01:14.500
So when x is equal
to 0-- and I'm just

01:14.500 --> 01:16.590
going to do it between
the interval 0 and 2 pi.

01:16.590 --> 01:17.964
Obvious it's a
periodic function,

01:17.964 --> 01:20.620
it'll keep going in the negative
and the positive directions.

01:20.620 --> 01:22.630
So what happens when
x is equal to 0?

01:22.630 --> 01:24.320
What is cosine of x?

01:24.320 --> 01:27.760
Well cosine of 0 is 1.

01:27.760 --> 01:35.270
What about when
x is equal to pi?

01:35.270 --> 01:37.632
What is cosine of pi?

01:37.632 --> 01:39.790
Well cosine of pi is negative 1.

01:42.360 --> 01:44.850
And then what's cosine of 2 pi?

01:44.850 --> 01:46.050
Well that's 1 again.

01:46.050 --> 01:48.510
We get back-- we've
completed a period,

01:48.510 --> 01:50.740
or we've completed
an entire cycle.

01:50.740 --> 01:54.580
And 2 pi is the
period of cosine of x.

01:54.580 --> 01:58.260
So this is one cycle
right over here.

01:58.260 --> 02:01.070
I could keep going if I wanted
to, but the whole point,

02:01.070 --> 02:06.140
I just wanted to graph this
one cycle between 0 and 2 pi.

02:06.140 --> 02:09.580
Now what I want to think about
is, what happens to this graph?

02:09.580 --> 02:12.620
Instead of graphing
y equals cosine

02:12.620 --> 02:15.260
of x-- let me draw
some graph paper again.

02:15.260 --> 02:18.640
Instead of drawing y is
equal to cosine of x,

02:18.640 --> 02:25.047
I'm going to draw y is
equal to cosine of 1/3 x.

02:25.047 --> 02:26.880
So the only difference
between that and that

02:26.880 --> 02:29.470
is now I'm multiplying
the x by 1/3.

02:29.470 --> 02:33.240
What's going to happen
to the graph over here?

02:33.240 --> 02:35.410
How is this going to change
instead of being an x,

02:35.410 --> 02:36.460
if it's a 1/3 x?

02:36.460 --> 02:38.845
What's going to
happen over here?

02:38.845 --> 02:40.970
And now I'm going to do it
over the entire interval

02:40.970 --> 02:41.840
between zero and six pi.

02:41.840 --> 02:43.715
So let me just make sure
I have enough space.

02:43.715 --> 02:49.370
So that's 3 pi, 4
pi, 5 pi, and 6 pi.

02:49.370 --> 02:52.660
What's going to
happen to this graph?

02:52.660 --> 02:54.740
Well, there's a couple of
ways to think about it.

02:54.740 --> 02:57.350
The easiest might
just be to say, well

02:57.350 --> 03:02.704
to complete an entire cycle,
we're going to go 1/3 as fast.

03:02.704 --> 03:04.370
Or we're going to go
three times slower.

03:04.370 --> 03:06.980
Or if you just want to
think about the period here,

03:06.980 --> 03:12.090
what's the period
of cosine of 1/3 x?

03:12.090 --> 03:16.860
Well the period is
going to be 2 pi divided

03:16.860 --> 03:19.540
by the absolute value of this
coefficient right over here.

03:19.540 --> 03:22.610
So it's the absolute value
of 1/3, which is just 1/3.

03:22.610 --> 03:25.280
So the period is 2
pi over 1/3, which

03:25.280 --> 03:29.140
is the same thing as 2 pi
times 3, which is 6 pi.

03:29.140 --> 03:31.780
Which gels with the intuition.

03:31.780 --> 03:34.160
That's going to take
three times as much

03:34.160 --> 03:37.370
time to get whatever we input
into the cosine function

03:37.370 --> 03:38.525
to get back to 2 pi.

03:38.525 --> 03:41.150
Because whatever we take
x, we're taking 1/3 of it.

03:41.150 --> 03:45.440
So to get to 2 pi, you can't
just have x equals 2 pi.

03:45.440 --> 03:48.740
x now has to equal 6
pi to get 2 pi inputted

03:48.740 --> 03:50.290
into the cosine function.

03:50.290 --> 03:53.590
So the period is now 6 pi.

03:53.590 --> 04:02.410
At x is equal to 0, 1/3 times 0
is 0, and the cosine of 0 is 1.

04:02.410 --> 04:06.990
When x is equal to 6 pi, you
have 6 pi divided by 3 is 2 pi.

04:06.990 --> 04:11.540
Cosine of 2 pi is equal to 1.

04:11.540 --> 04:14.120
And if you want to go
in between, over here

04:14.120 --> 04:15.520
to go in between, we tried pi.

04:15.520 --> 04:17.410
But over here, we
could try 3 pi.

04:17.410 --> 04:21.420
When x is 3 pi, you
have cosine of 1/3

04:21.420 --> 04:23.550
of 3 pi, that's cosine of pi.

04:23.550 --> 04:26.210
Cosine of pi is negative 1.

04:26.210 --> 04:32.140
So when x is equal to 3 pi,
we have cosine of 1/3 times

04:32.140 --> 04:34.350
3 pi is negative 1.

04:34.350 --> 04:36.335
So it's going to look
something like this.

04:41.540 --> 04:45.170
Trying my best attempt.

04:45.170 --> 04:47.020
to draw it.

04:47.020 --> 04:49.690
So it's going to look
something like this.

04:49.690 --> 04:52.750
So you see, to go from y
equals cosine of x to y

04:52.750 --> 04:57.210
equals cosine of 1/3 x, it
essentially stretched out

04:57.210 --> 05:00.190
to this function
by a factor of 3.

05:00.190 --> 05:03.050
You can see this period
is three times longer.

05:03.050 --> 05:06.520
The period here was 2 pi.

05:10.260 --> 05:12.830
All right, well there's
only one more transformation

05:12.830 --> 05:14.980
we need in order to
get to the function

05:14.980 --> 05:16.190
that they're asking us about.

05:16.190 --> 05:18.830
We just have to, instead of
having a cosine of 1/3 x,

05:18.830 --> 05:22.530
we just have to negative
2.5 cosine of 1/3 x.

05:22.530 --> 05:23.710
So let's try to draw that.

05:23.710 --> 05:29.260
So let me put my
axis here again.

05:29.260 --> 05:32.700
And let me label it.

05:32.700 --> 05:43.050
So that's 2 pi, 3 pi,
4 pi, 5 pi, and 6 pi.

05:43.050 --> 05:48.760
And our goal now is to draw
the graph of y is equal to--

05:48.760 --> 05:51.106
and we're just doing it over
between 0 and 6 pi here.

05:51.106 --> 05:52.730
We only did it between
0 and 2 pi here.

05:52.730 --> 05:58.460
Obviously they're all periodic,
they all keep going on and on.

05:58.460 --> 06:03.410
But now we want to graph y is
equal to negative 2.5 times

06:03.410 --> 06:08.290
cosine of 1/3 x.

06:08.290 --> 06:12.360
So given this change, we're now
multiplying by negative 2.5,

06:12.360 --> 06:14.040
what is going to
be-- well actually,

06:14.040 --> 06:15.350
let's think about a few things.

06:15.350 --> 06:18.150
What was the amplitude
in the first two graphs

06:18.150 --> 06:19.010
right over here?

06:26.824 --> 06:28.490
Well there's two ways
to think about it.

06:28.490 --> 06:30.940
You could say the amplitude
is half the difference

06:30.940 --> 06:33.090
between the minimum
and the maximum points.

06:33.090 --> 06:35.350
In either of these case,
the minimum is negative 1,

06:35.350 --> 06:36.940
maximum is 1.

06:36.940 --> 06:40.910
The difference is 2,
half of that is 1.

06:40.910 --> 06:43.040
Or you could just say
it's the absolute value

06:43.040 --> 06:45.620
of the coefficient here,
which is implicitly a 1.

06:45.620 --> 06:50.130
And the absolute value
of 1 is, once again, 1.

06:50.130 --> 06:54.810
What's going to be the amplitude
for this thing right over here?

06:54.810 --> 06:59.520
Well the amplitude is going to
be the absolute value of what's

06:59.520 --> 07:01.540
multiplying the cosine function.

07:01.540 --> 07:05.500
So the amplitude in this
case, do it in green,

07:05.500 --> 07:09.710
the amplitude is going to be
equal to the absolute value

07:09.710 --> 07:15.150
of negative 2.5,
which is equal to 2.5.

07:15.150 --> 07:20.620
So given that, how is
multiplying by negative 2.5

07:20.620 --> 07:26.990
going to transform this
graph right over here?

07:26.990 --> 07:28.200
Well let's think about it.

07:28.200 --> 07:31.700
If it was multiplying
by just a positive 2.5,

07:31.700 --> 07:33.140
you would stretch it out.

07:33.140 --> 07:37.020
At each point it would go
up by a factor of 2 and 1/2.

07:37.020 --> 07:39.565
But it's a negative
2.5, so at each point,

07:39.565 --> 07:41.190
you're going to
stretch it out and then

07:41.190 --> 07:43.580
you're going to flip
it over the x-axis.

07:43.580 --> 07:44.860
So let's do that.

07:44.860 --> 07:49.900
So when x was 0, you
got 1 in this case.

07:49.900 --> 07:52.550
But now we're going to
multiply that by negative 2.5,

07:52.550 --> 07:55.180
which means you're going
to get to negative 2.5.

07:55.180 --> 07:57.080
So let me draw negative
2.5 right over there.

07:57.080 --> 07:59.280
So that's negative 2.5.

07:59.280 --> 08:01.090
That'd be negative--
let me make it clear.

08:01.090 --> 08:04.460
This would be negative
3 right over here,

08:04.460 --> 08:06.310
this would be positive 3.

08:06.310 --> 08:09.291
So that number right over
there is negative 2.5.

08:09.291 --> 08:10.790
And let me draw a
dotted line there.

08:10.790 --> 08:13.390
It could serve to be useful.

08:13.390 --> 08:21.226
Now when cosine
of 1/3 x is 0, it

08:21.226 --> 08:22.850
doesn't matter what
you multiply it by,

08:22.850 --> 08:27.020
you're still going to
get 0 right over here.

08:27.020 --> 08:30.530
Now, when cosine of
1/3 x was negative 1,

08:30.530 --> 08:34.980
which was the case when x
is equal to 3 pi, what's

08:34.980 --> 08:38.030
going to happen over here?

08:38.030 --> 08:41.020
Well cosine of 1/3 x,
we see, is negative 1.

08:41.020 --> 08:45.330
Negative 1 times negative
2.5 is positive 2.5.

08:45.330 --> 08:48.300
So we're going to get to
positive 2.5, which is right--

08:48.300 --> 08:50.030
let me draw a dotted
line over here.

08:50.030 --> 08:54.340
We're going to get to positive
2.5, which is right over there.

08:54.340 --> 08:58.580
And then when cosine
of 1/3 x is equal to 0,

08:58.580 --> 09:02.700
doesn't matter what we
multiply it by, we get to 0.

09:02.700 --> 09:09.940
And then finally, when x
is at 6 pi, cosine of 1/3 x

09:09.940 --> 09:12.090
is equal to 1.

09:12.090 --> 09:15.460
What's that going to be when
you multiply it by negative 2.5?

09:15.460 --> 09:17.520
Well it's going to
be negative 2.5.

09:17.520 --> 09:19.720
So we're going to
get back over here.

09:19.720 --> 09:21.560
So we're ready to
draw our graph.

09:21.560 --> 09:24.320
It looks something-- let
me do that in magenta color

09:24.320 --> 09:26.620
since that's what the
color I wrote this in.

09:26.620 --> 09:27.800
It will look like this.

09:31.000 --> 09:33.640
I can draw it as a solid line.

09:33.640 --> 09:39.740
So it will look like that.

09:39.740 --> 09:41.050
So you saw what happened.

09:41.050 --> 09:44.930
By putting this 1/3 here,
it stretched out the graph.

09:44.930 --> 09:47.540
It increased the period
by a factor of 3.

09:47.540 --> 09:50.760
And then multiplying
it by negative 2.5--

09:50.760 --> 09:54.290
if you just multiply
it by 2.5, you

09:54.290 --> 10:00.640
would just multiply
that out a little bit.

10:00.640 --> 10:02.140
But now it's a
negative, so not only

10:02.140 --> 10:05.690
do you increase the amplitude,
but you flip it over.

10:05.690 --> 10:09.290
So it is, indeed, the case
that the amplitude here is 2.5.

10:09.290 --> 10:11.437
We vary 2.5 from
our middle position.

10:11.437 --> 10:13.770
Or you could say that the
difference between the minimum

10:13.770 --> 10:16.890
and the maximum is 5,
so half of that is 2.5.

10:16.890 --> 10:19.515
But it isn't just multiplying
this graph by 2.5.

10:19.515 --> 10:21.620
If you multiply
this graph by 2.5,

10:21.620 --> 10:24.770
you'd get something-- let
me be a little neater.

10:24.770 --> 10:32.160
You would get something that
looked something like that.

10:32.160 --> 10:34.460
But because we
had a negative, we

10:34.460 --> 10:36.480
had to flip it over the x-axis.

10:36.480 --> 10:37.370
And we got this here.

10:37.370 --> 10:39.810
So this amplitude
is 2.5, but it's

10:39.810 --> 10:43.440
a flipped over
version of this graph.
